WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.414
Six Moves, from EL Publishing. Move five: check without asking do you understand.

2
00:00:05.864 --> 00:00:10.101
You finish explaining. You ask, does everyone understand? Everyone nods.

3
00:00:10.101 --> 00:00:15.691
The newcomer nods hardest, because nodding is the one thing they can do perfectly. You move on.

4
00:00:15.691 --> 00:00:16.750
Nobody understood.

5
00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:22.155
Do you understand fails for three reasons. Yes is the polite answer in every language.

6
00:00:22.155 --> 00:00:25.670
Answering honestly requires English the student may not have.

7
00:00:25.670 --> 00:00:30.682
And it checks one student, or none — a nod from thirty people is no information at all.

8
00:00:31.132 --> 00:00:36.553
The move: check in a way that doesn't require English to answer, and that every student answers at once.

9
00:00:37.003 --> 00:00:42.193
Point to the nucleus on your diagram. Thirty fingers. You can see in two seconds who has it.

10
00:00:42.643 --> 00:00:48.226
Is this a plant cell or an animal cell? One finger for plant, two for animal. Hold them up.

11
00:00:48.676 --> 00:00:53.422
Sort these six cards into plant and animal. A level one can do it without a word.

12
00:00:53.872 --> 00:00:57.608
Draw the process in three boxes. No sentences required.

13
00:00:58.058 --> 00:01:04.364
Finish my sentence. The nucleus controls the — and they say, cell. That's participation. It builds up.

14
00:01:04.814 --> 00:01:12.384
Every one of those is answered by every student at the same moment, without a sentence of English, and you get a read on the whole room in ten seconds.

15
00:01:12.834 --> 00:01:16.996
And when you do ask a question out loud, make the question carry the words.

16
00:01:16.996 --> 00:01:22.156
Instead of what does the nucleus do, ask: does the nucleus control the cell, or store energy?

17
00:01:22.156 --> 00:01:25.375
Now the student has the words. They say, control the cell.

18
00:01:25.375 --> 00:01:29.814
Next week you ask the open question, and they've heard themselves say it before.

19
00:01:30.264 --> 00:01:33.456
Then do something with what you saw. If they have it, move on.

20
00:01:33.456 --> 00:01:38.758
If half the room missed it, one more example — in a different channel: a picture, a gesture, the board.

21
00:01:38.758 --> 00:01:43.237
If the newcomer missed it, their partner's job is to show them, not translate for them.

22
00:01:43.687 --> 00:01:47.684
Tomorrow: replace one do you understand with point to it.

23
00:01:47.684 --> 00:01:53.223
Six Moves is in the PD Library at radan55 dot github dot io slash everyteacher.
